4	
5	- **The GLB as it comes:** a low-poly export.
6	  - 5,498 triangles, split into about 1,000 loose panels, 1.0 m long, muzzle towards -Y, up +Z.
7	  - One 4096 albedo with baked light, smears and soft edges, and a `neon_mask`. No roughness, metallic, normal or AO map.
8	  - Top rail teeth of uneven length and spacing.
9	- **`Rifle.blend` is rebuilt from it** by `Tools/Blender/rifle_build_blend.py` (the first version was the plain GLB import, and the rifle read soft in the game):
10	  - SHA-256 `40E6E427585D336CE81450E383948B4F669DF6C141B20ECD849515EB7BB6D870` (Blender 5.3.0);
11	  - vertices welded, Tripo's normals dropped, hard edges above 30°, face-area weighted normals;
12	  - `T_Rifle_BaseColor`: one flat tone per panel (white polymer, two greys, dark metal) and the small drawn details re-cut crisp; the barrel, front sight, eyepiece and the band beside the rail are forced to dark metal as whole parts, because their panels came out as patches of different tones;
13	  - `T_Rifle_ORM`: AO baked in Cycles, roughness and metallic by tone;
14	  - `T_Rifle_Normal`: baked in Cycles (OpenGL): rounded edges and the drawn details pressed in;
15	  - `T_Rifle_EmissiveMask`: the neon mask with its edges re-cut, and without the GLB's ragged side strip on the handguard;
16	  - modelled by the script, in the flat metal slot `Rifle_Rail`:
17	    - the top rail: the old one deleted, 23 equal teeth (10 mm, 6.4 mm gaps), chamfered;
18	    - an energy bar on each side of the handguard, on its upper sloped face: a dark bezel 238 mm long with a straight glowing strip (the strip uses the atlas slot);
19	    - the ammo screen's housing on the rear of the sight's eyepiece, tilted 14° up towards the player;
20	  - also modelled by the script: the hologram pane (32 × 16 mm, in the housing) and the magazine (the GLB has none: a curved box, base plate and a glowing slit on each side);
21	  - objects `Rifle_Body`, `Rifle_Trigger`, `Rifle_Lens`, `Rifle_AmmoDisplay`, `Rifle_Magazine`. Pivots, the magazine's slide-out vector and the strip's span are custom properties on them, read by the export.
22	  - To rebuild: `blender -b --factory-startup --python Tools/Blender/rifle_build_blend.py -- <model.glb> Rifle.blend` (about 3 minutes, most of it the two bakes).
23	- **One rifle, one source** (owner's rule): `Rifle.blend` is the only source and `/Game/Glyphbound/Weapons/Rifle` the only result. `Tools/Jam/Import-Rifle.ps1` (Editor closed, pistol imported first):
24	  - exports the five parts and the four textures (`rifle_export_fbx.py`) into a temporary folder under `Saved/`;
25	  - imports them with their materials (`import_rifle.py`);
26	  - deletes the folder.
27	- The parts:
28	  - body, with the modelled rail, energy bars and screen housing;
29	  - trigger, picked by the box of its panels, origin at its hinge;
30	  - magazine, modelled;
31	  - weak-point light: the two oval lenses of the sight;
32	  - ammo display: a 2:1 hologram pane in the housing behind the sight's eyepiece.
33	- Energy strip: in the body's second UV map (`ImbueU`):
34	  - U runs from 0 at the muzzle end of the energy bars' strips to 1 at their rear end;
35	  - in Blender, V = 1 on the strip and 0 off it, flipped by the import;
36	  - `M_RifleAtlas` cuts the strip into 13 cells that drain like the pistol's bar.
37	- `export_report.json` is written by each import: the offsets and motion the game code uses (`AGBGun`).

